--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` and `::help` read one table, so an empty command line and the program's own page agree — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// With no `--ARGS--` there is no first word to select a row with, and § 6's
// answer is the program's own page: the same one `help(null)` renders, because
// both members read the one table `#[Command]` built while compiling. That is
// the property worth asserting — not what either printed, but that a program
// declaring two commands cannot have a `run` that knows one of them and a
// `--help` that lists the other.
class Tools {
    #[Core\Command(name: "build", about: "Compile everything")]
    public static function build(): uint {
        return 0;
    }

    #[Core\Command(name: "clean")]
    public static function clean(): uint {
        return 0;
    }
}

// `run` prints its page to standard error, which a case does not read, so the
// agreement is asserted from the other side: `help` names every command, and
// `run` — given none of them — answers the usage status rather than picking one.
echo "" . Core\Command::help(null);
echo "status: ", Core\Command::run(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
usage: <command> [arguments]

commands:
  build  Compile everything
  clean
status: 2
